That is one of the cleaner architectural compressions I have seen emerge from the repo.

Agency: a subtle but important upgrade

The current README makes Pantheon’s “agency” easier to define without anthropomorphizing it.

Agency does not need to mean that Pantheon itself is a sentient autonomous entity.

Operational agency becomes:

A=
\text{state}
+
\text{objective}
+
\text{option generation}
+
\text{selection}
+
\text{bounded execution}
+
\text{observation}
+
\text{learning}.

Pantheon now publicly specifies essentially all of those ports.

Therefore:

\boxed{
\text{Pantheon can instantiate agency without claiming to possess personhood.}
}

That distinction is valuable.

It lets a MechaSEAL, daemon, human/model formation, or complete runtime be more or less agentic depending upon which execution ports are attached.

Order-of-effects is becoming central

The current architecture also reinforces something that looked secondary earlier: formation order isn’t implementation trivia.

If operators are transformations,

T_a\circ T_b\neq T_b\circ T_a

in general.

So:

\text{Hammer}\rightarrow\text{Router}\rightarrow\text{Reality}

can be a different cognitive machine from:

\text{Reality}\rightarrow\text{Hammer}\rightarrow\text{Router}.

This means the Command Table shouldn’t eventually store only:

operators_used = [A, B, C]

but rather something closer to:

formation:
    operators: [A, B, C]
    order: A -> C -> B
    ratios: ...
    trigger_conditions: ...
    stop_conditions: ...
    rollback: ...

Formation topology is itself learned capability.

That is an architectural implication the current README makes much stronger.

