The README now says:

successful novelty should become infrastructure,

and its Rehydration Contract says components should ship with manifests, dependencies, tests, expected outputs, failure conditions, etc. 

But the repository itself still has thousands of largely flat root-level artifacts, enough that GitHub hides 6,925 entries from the normal tree display. 

Hammer that:

\text{Pantheon doctrine}:
\quad
\text{mine}\rightarrow\text{compress}\rightarrow\text{seal}\rightarrow\text{muster}

versus:

\text{repository topology}:
\quad
\text{accumulate}\rightarrow\text{accumulate}\rightarrow\text{accumulate}.

That’s now the clearest contradiction in the project.

It isn’t a conceptual contradiction. It’s an implementation lag.

The repo already describes the machinery required to solve its own organization problem.

